# Reference of the settings a local .env can override. Copy it only if you want
# one; ./run_dev.sh runs a checkout that has no .env at all. Do NOT commit .env.
#
# The two secrets the app refuses to start without on a deployed host, the session
# signing key and the web-to-worker key, are deliberately absent from this file.
# Development takes a fixed fallback for each from the config loader, which is
# safe because neither authenticates anything beyond your own machine. The
# compose stack is the exception: it runs NODE_ENV=production for parity, so the
# fallbacks do not apply there and its launcher generates a pair per run.
#
# The four values below carry the same defaults in the config loader, so setting
# them changes nothing unless you want different ones. On a deployed host the
# loader requires all four; the deploy syncs the log level from Parameter Store,
# the port is a compose literal, and the database path and public base URL are
# host values set at bring-up.

COMPOSE_FILE=docker/docker-compose.yml
PORT=3000
NODE_ENV=development
LOG_LEVEL=info
FOOTBAG_DB_PATH=./database/footbag.db
PUBLIC_BASE_URL=http://localhost:3000

# Deploy smoke-check targets are not read from here. The post-deploy smoke
# resolves the address it probes from terraform output
# (`terraform -chdir=terraform/<environment> output -raw cloudfront_domain`), and
# a production deploy verifies the staging smoke gate the same way before it
# proceeds, so the unpublished staging address needs no copy on the workstation.
# SMOKE_BASE_URL overrides the resolved value for a single run. Nothing in this
# file is needed in order to deploy.

# Express trust-proxy setting. Controls how req.ip and rate-limit keys resolve
# through X-Forwarded-For. Accepts an integer (hop count), 'true'/'false', or
# a comma-separated subnet list. Default in production: the named-range string
# 'loopback, linklocal, uniquelocal' (trusts loopback, link-local, and the
# private subnets that include the docker bridge). Default elsewhere: 0.
# Leave unset for sensible defaults.
# TRUST_PROXY=loopback, linklocal, uniquelocal

# SES_ADAPTER selects the email transport. 'stub' captures messages in memory
# and surfaces them on email-gated pages via the in-page simulated-email card;
# 'live' sends through AWS SES. Dev and staging run 'stub'; production derives
# the value from its email arming switch at deploy (armed -> live,
# dark -> stub). SES_FROM_IDENTITY is required only when SES_ADAPTER=live.
# SES_ADAPTER=stub

# Arming switches ('armed' | 'dark'), one per real-world side effect. On
# production the deploy syncs each from its SSM app/* parameter and derives
# the matching adapter: 'dark' keeps the side on its stub (staging-like, no
# mail delivered, no money moved), 'armed' selects the live adapter with every
# strict check. Mandatory-explicit under prod-mode boots; inert below
# production, where the stub mandates apply regardless (default 'armed').
# EMAIL_SEND_ARMED=armed
# PAYMENTS_ARMED=armed

# Two more arming switches, same shape and one difference: these are NOT inert
# below production. Screening a member-submitted link and probing whether its
# host answers have no real-world side effect to withhold from a lower
# environment, so every deployed environment derives its adapter from these
# (armed -> live, dark -> the non-probing value). A local run keeps the stub
# adapters and these defaults, so nothing here needs setting to work locally.
# URL_SCREENING_ARMED=armed
# REACHABILITY_ARMED=armed

# SES_FROM_IDENTITY is the verified SES sender address (e.g. noreply@footbag.org).
# Required when SES_ADAPTER=live; unused in stub mode. Production fails fast if unset.
# SES_FROM_IDENTITY=noreply@footbag.org

# Bounce, complaint and alarm notifications are read from queues rather than
# pushed to this application, so there is no shared secret to set here. The
# queue URLs are written onto a deployed host from the Terraform outputs by
# scripts/set-host-env.sh; locally they are unset and the feeds are inert.
# SES_FEEDBACK_QUEUE_URL=
# ALARM_QUEUE_URL=

# Image worker (Sharp) HTTP boundary. The web app POSTs uploaded image bytes
# to this URL; the worker returns processed thumb/display variants. Required
# in production; defaults to http://localhost:4001 in dev (matches `npm run
# dev:image`). IMAGE_PORT is read by the worker process itself; IMAGE_PROCESSOR_URL
# is read by the web. IMAGE_MAX_CONCURRENT bounds simultaneous Sharp ops in the
# worker (default 2). IMAGE_PROCESS_TIMEOUT_MS is the web-side request timeout
# (default 30000).
# IMAGE_PROCESSOR_URL=http://localhost:4001
# IMAGE_PORT=4000
# IMAGE_MAX_CONCURRENT=2
# IMAGE_PROCESS_TIMEOUT_MS=30000

# Curator video limits. These three are sized against each other: the encoder
# must be able to finish a file at VIDEO_MAX_BYTES within FFMPEG_TIMEOUT_SECONDS
# on the production host, or the advertised limit fails at the top of its range.
#
# FFMPEG_TIMEOUT_SECONDS bounds one ffmpeg run. A malformed input can put the
# encoder into a loop that consumes a core and produces nothing; unbounded, that
# holds the worker's video slot forever and the media job neither completes nor
# fails. It must be less than VIDEO_TRANSCODE_TIMEOUT_MS or the caller gives up
# and marks the job failed while ffmpeg keeps running and keeps the slot; boot
# refuses the inverted ordering rather than letting it degrade silently.
#
# VIDEO_X264_PRESET trades encode speed against compression efficiency and is
# the cheapest lever if the limit needs raising. Measured on 720p material at
# two threads: medium 3.6x realtime, faster 7.0x, veryfast 11.8x. Production
# runs two vCPUs, so expect appreciably less than those figures.
#
# VIDEO_MAX_HEIGHT caps output height and never upscales, so a source at or
# below it passes through unscaled. It is the other lever on encode cost:
# measured on one source at the same preset, 1080p costs about 2.1x what 720p
# does, and 4K several times more again. Dropping it to 720 roughly doubles the
# duration that fits in the same time budget.
# VIDEO_MAX_BYTES=125829120
# VIDEO_MAX_HEIGHT=1080
# FFMPEG_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=240
# VIDEO_X264_PRESET=faster

# INTERNAL_EVENT_SECRET authenticates web↔image-worker and web↔transcode-worker
# HTTP traffic (x-internal-secret header on /process/* and /ipc/job-events).
# Required in every environment, and the same value must reach web and both
# workers. Development takes a fixed fallback from the config loader, so all
# three agree with nothing configured. The compose stack runs NODE_ENV=production
# and so does not get that fallback; its launcher generates one per run and
# exports it to every container. Set your own here only if you need a fixed value.
# INTERNAL_EVENT_SECRET=

# Media storage adapter. 'local' writes processed JPEGs and curator media bytes
# to the local filesystem under the configured upload directory; 's3' PUTs to
# MEDIA_STORAGE_S3_BUCKET in AWS_REGION. Required in production (no default;
# fail-fast). Defaults to 'local' in dev. When set to 's3',
# MEDIA_STORAGE_S3_BUCKET and AWS_REGION must also be set, and the bucket must
# be readable by the CloudFront distribution via OAC.
# MEDIA_STORAGE_ADAPTER=local
# MEDIA_STORAGE_S3_BUCKET=

# SECRETS_ADAPTER resolves runtime secrets (e.g. the Safe Browsing key). 'stub' is an
# in-memory map and is the default in dev and test: every consuming adapter runs its
# own stub locally, so nothing asks it for a value. 'live' reads SSM SecureString
# parameters under the FOOTBAG_ENV-derived prefix. Production must set this explicitly
# (no default); FOOTBAG_ENV is then required to derive the SSM prefix. There is no
# file-backed option: a credential file on a workstation sits outside every rotation
# this project runs.
# SECRETS_ADAPTER=stub

# Safe Browsing v4 API. Default in dev: stub adapter pre-seeded with the
# canonical Google malware test URL — submitting that URL in any external-link
# form rejects with "This URL is not allowed." without an outbound call. Exercising
# the live Google API locally means pointing SECRETS_ADAPTER at 'live' with a
# configured AWS profile, so the key is read from Parameter Store rather than held on
# the workstation. See docs/DEV_ONBOARDING.md §1.11 for the full opt-in walkthrough.
# On a deployed host this is not hand-set: the deploy derives it from the URL
# screening arming switch, and a boot where the two disagree is refused.
# SAFE_BROWSING_ADAPTER=live

# Payment adapter selector. 'stub' simulates Stripe end-to-end in-process
# (programmable success/failure outcomes via the test API; member walks
# through a real-looking confirmation page). 'live' wraps the Stripe SDK and
# boots only on production; below production the stub is the sole bootable
# value. Production must set this explicitly and derives it from the payments
# arming switch at deploy (armed -> live, dark -> stub). Default in
# non-production is 'stub'.
# PAYMENT_ADAPTER=stub

# HTTP_REACHABILITY_ADAPTER controls outbound URL-reachability probes from the
# validation path. 'stub' (default in non-production) and 'disabled' make no
# outbound calls; 'live' performs real probes. Production has no default and
# fails fast at boot if unset. On a deployed host this is not hand-set either:
# the deploy derives it from the reachability arming switch, and 'stub' is a
# development value that no deployed host holds.
# HTTP_REACHABILITY_ADAPTER=stub

# Stripe webhook signing secret. Only consumed when PAYMENT_ADAPTER=live, where
# the verifier validates inbound Stripe-Signature headers against it. In stub
# mode (the default) it is unused: the stub adapter signs and verifies synthetic
# events with its own co-located whsec_stub secret. For local live testing, run
# `stripe listen --forward-to localhost:3000/payments/webhook` and paste the
# printed whsec_ here. Production sources this from SSM; production rejects a
# whsec_stub-prefixed value.
# STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET=

# The outgoing webhook signing secret, set only while a rotation is in flight.
# Stripe's secret roll signs every delivery with both the old and the new secret
# for the length of the roll window; accepting both is what lets the secret
# change without dropping deliveries. Set this to the old secret when you roll,
# then clear it once the window closes. Production rejects a whsec_stub-prefixed
# value here for the same reason it does for the current secret.
# STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET_PREVIOUS=

